# Auto commit, push, and notify

After finishing a **substantial** change (feature, bugfix, multi-file edit, config, docs, adaptive/video work — not a one-line typo or pure Q&A), do **all** of the following in the **same turn**, without waiting for the user to ask:

1. **Write an English commit message** (Latin script only, imperative, ~1 line, under ~72 chars).
2. **Create the git commit** (stage relevant files only).
3. **Push** to the remote (`git push -u origin HEAD` or current tracking branch).
4. **Tell the user** that commit + push were done (include short message + commit hash when possible).

## Required sequence

```text
git status / git diff / git log   (parallel)
git add <relevant files>
git commit -m "<English message>"
git push origin HEAD
→ reply to user: committed + pushed
```

On Windows PowerShell do **not** use bash HEREDOC; use:

```powershell
git commit -m "English commit message here."
```

## Commit message rules

- **English only** (no Cyrillic)
- Short, imperative: `Raise SFU bitrates and add manual session quality controls`
- Focus on why/what in one phrase

## Do not auto-commit when

- User is only asking a question / reviewing / exploring
- Working tree has unrelated WIP the user did not ask to include
- Change is trivial (typo, comment-only) unless user asked to ship it
- Commit would include secrets (`.env`, credentials, keys)

## Safety

- Never `--force` push to `main`/`master`
- Never amend a commit that was already pushed
- Never update git config
- Never skip hooks unless user explicitly asks
- Never commit `.env` / secrets / credentials
